layout: null
---
{% comment %}
  feed.json — the same twenty posts as feed.xml, as JSON.

  The Corpus alerts Worker (`workers/alerts/worker.js` in C:\CORPUS) builds one weekly
  email per reader and carries the main site's new writing as one section of it. A
  Worker has no XML parser and pulling one in to read a feed this site also writes as
  a Liquid template is work for nothing, so the feed is written twice. `feed.xml` stays
  exactly as it was, for feed readers.

  The fields are the four the digest uses and no more: `title`, `url`, `date` and
  `description`. `date` is `YYYY-MM-DD`, because the Worker's send window is a pair of
  UTC days and a comparison between two of those strings is the whole of the test.
  The description falls through the same four sources feed.xml uses, in the same order,
  so the two files never disagree about what a post says.

  `jsonify` does the escaping. A title with a quote or a backslash in it is the reason
  this is not string concatenation.
{% endcomment %}
{
  "title": {{ site.title | jsonify }},
  "url": {{ site.url | jsonify }},
  "built": {{ site.time | date: "%Y-%m-%d" | jsonify }},
  "items": [
{% for item in site.posts limit:20 %}
    {
      "title": {{ item.title | jsonify }},
      "url": {{ item.url | absolute_url | jsonify }},
      "date": {{ item.date | date: "%Y-%m-%d" | jsonify }},
      "description": {% if item.description %}{{ item.description | jsonify }}{% elsif item.subtitle %}{{ item.subtitle | jsonify }}{% elsif item.summary %}{{ item.summary | jsonify }}{% else %}{{ item.content | strip_html | truncate: 280 | jsonify }}{% endif %}
    }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
{% endfor %}
  ]
}
